						 <div style="text-align: right;"></div> <p>His family calls him Leroy, his friends call him Elroy (L. Roy), his acquaintances call him Lee, nobody calls him Roy, and his enemies call him Carl.</p> <p>Dr. Carlson is not, nor has he ever been, affiliated with the Shark Punching Center in any way, shape, or form. He will continue saying it until you believe him. No one has ever once seen him punching a shark, and even if they did what of it? Lots of people hate sharks! They're dangerous and scary! Stop looking at him like that! He loves it here at the SPC!</p> <p>No, please, he meant to say SCP, he swears! It's an easy mistake to make! Don't&#8230;</p> <p><em>Dr. Carlson is currently unavailable. Please leave a message after the cognitohazard.</em></p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Testing logs that Dr. Carlson has contributed to.</strong> Some of them actually do involve sharks and/or punching, but that doesn't mean anything!</p> <ul> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/experiment-log-t-98816-oc108-682">Experiment Log T-98816-OC108/682</a>, anything with his name on it, plus 101. The non-anomalous tests are my favorite because I think we're starting to lose focus on our actual goal here and just crosstesting for the crosstest god. Of course, that <em>is</em> the intention of the article, so whatever. There was another Carlson/SPC joke here, but it was removed.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-1459-extended-testing-log">SCP-1459 Extended Testing Log</a>, anything with his name on it. Trying to do some actual science here to learn about the thing's properties and origins, but a little bit of fooling around too. There used to be one where Dr. Carlson causes a PROTOCOL 4000-ESHU breach, but it was removed.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-978-extended-test-logs">SCP-978 Extended Test Logs</a> - SCP-134, SCP-076-2, SCP-466, D-11424, D-4892, Talloran, and Kondraki. I really wish this log was in chronological order like literally every other test log on the website.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/document-507-3b">Document 507-3B</a> - the one with the apple seeds, the one he slept through, and the one he missed because he blinked.</li> </ul> </blockquote> <blockquote> <p><strong>Things Dr. Carlson has not punched,</strong> and wouldn't punch even if they were sharks:</p> <ul> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-4225">SCP-4225</a> - Sammy the Superfluous Serpent, by Sr. Supertainment. First (and so far only) installment in a series of Amazing Alliterative Animals, a less successful sequel series to Wondertainment's award-winning Little Misters line of toys.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-4314">SCP-4314</a> - An Irrational Number. A pattern screamer hidden in the digits of pi, very poorly executed because I don't understand math very well. Will rewrite this sonuva eventually, possibly after a deletion.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-4820">SCP-4820</a> - The Custom of the Sea. My most popular article, because people just can't get enough autocannibalism. Really sucked at the beginning because of unnecessary Sarkic bullshit, but it's much better now. Probably should have let it get deleted and repost to shed the downvotes from the bad versions, but it's too late now.</li> </ul> </blockquote> <blockquote> <p><strong>Stories that are not about Dr. Carlson punching sharks:</strong></p> <ul> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/halloween-on-17th-street">Halloween on 17th Street</a> - my entry for the Halloween2018 contest, about what happens when you tell an 8-year-old Type Green about Halloween. Didn't do great in the contest, but I like it.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/the-drooling-path">The Drooling Path</a> - a series tying together some supremely creepy but largely underused SCPs, including some of my personal favorites. Starring <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-058">SCP-058</a>, a slightly unhinged researcher from the memetics division, and a research assistant named James.</li> </ul> </blockquote> <blockquote> <p><strong>Other things people have accused Dr. Carlson of being involved with:</strong></p> <ul> <li>I added an iron maiden that plays &quot;Iron Maiden&quot;, from Iron Maiden's album <em>Iron Maiden</em>, to one of the anomalous object logs. Not sure if it's still there, can't be bothered to check. More recently added a CD full of death/black metal covers of Stairway to Heaven that never existed.</li> <li><a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/interviews-with-prospective-groups-of-interest">Interviews with Prospective Groups of Interest</a>. I blame Clef. I once had an entry here for the RPC too, but was informed that that was both against the author's wishes and in extremely bad taste.</li> </ul> </blockquote> <blockquote> <p><strong>Dr. Carlson's greatest failures.</strong> Their untimely deaths were not due to being punched.</p> <ul> <li>SCP-5081 - PACt-MAN. An Atari Arcadia clone of Pac-Man with a demon and some souls trapped inside it. You can soak quarters in the blood of murder victims and trade their souls to the demon in exchange for wishes. Didn't turn out well on the site for reasons I can't even remember, but I haven't given up on it.</li> <li>SCP-5930-EX - Pablo Neruda's (formerly) toxic mongoose. Pablo Neruda's pet mongoose, who'd been implanted with a poison capsule in a failed attempt to assassinate him. The Foundation thought the mongoose was anomalously sweating poison, and by the time they figured out what was actually going on it was no longer plausible to give him his pet back, so they kept it and made it the Site pet. This was my entry in the Exquisite Corpse contest, so I claim no more than 50% of the blame for its failure. My part was pretty bad, though.</li> <li>I once asked Clef if he liked my idea for an <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-166">SCP-166</a> rewrite, but he never responded, probably because I'm too new to be restoring the works of the masters. The one that did happen was better than mine anyway, though.</li> <li>A Closer Examination of SCP-173. A tale compiling some interesting conclusions about 173 based on test results and deductive reasoning. Still needs some work, but I haven't given up.</li> </ul> </blockquote> <p>by <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kothardarastrix" ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3460794&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609575643" alt="Kothardarastrix" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3460794)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kothardarastrix" >Kothardarastrix</a></span></p> 
